7592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1031 "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1033 1868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1034 0.5951061045948267 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7821b684-8118-5d42-9459-ebb20010e0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124325 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124325 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/3d/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87580982-1d5a-5f68-b9c8-df77b4da0196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124325 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](images/5f/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](images/f9/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](images/ca/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124326 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124326 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation ![](images/a4/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3694e9e-804f-55f0-9fb1-a78caec29224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124326 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124327 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124327 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/d0/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](images/4d/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc0725a9-354c-5e01-a924-12c0d2723ba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124327 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124355 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124355 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation \n![](images/c3/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](images/4f/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124355 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ba5ded2-bfca-5e53-9056-8aede726505f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124355 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124516 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124516 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124516 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921861251