2 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323602 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323602 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323602 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323602 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da7e412b-267f-58a3-94eb-d3421d219bcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323616 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df87c177-5600-5668-aa22-f776774133ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323931 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{aligned}]$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2204189b-4707-5bbc-8475-ce3acaaee8eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323931 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323943 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323943 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d0e70ad-1c81-5568-a326-e79afb6bb189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323943 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323944 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1546ae0c-0405-54cb-8c7c-cf6c58a51fc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323944 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348504 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348504 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348504 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "065fe2d2-27bc-5b0d-a756-2e96d9074922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348842 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348842 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348842 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d10ef55-04cd-5945-9adf-137539253155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349011 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349011 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e0f2a4d-8be3-5c94-a0eb-8fa431f10e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349208 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53750d24-755f-51fd-b4b1-3999616bb4bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349221 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "54c76c9c-3dfe-5f84-83f4-73a8745d7382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349293 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218