2186351146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351146 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351146 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351146 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186348504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351146 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8c54f7c-daab-59c3-8960-8e6c25c2f170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351301 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351301 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "328b0afd-0c82-5cff-b5a3-0cdd36c80b90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351301 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot k-4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3k+12 \\\\ 2k-8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3k+12=0 \\\\ 2k-8=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351303 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351303 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351303 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351303 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 16y-6x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351303 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae878865-22de-5344-874b-fc28f0188962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351303 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3=2\\cdot 1-3=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351304 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351304 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351304 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351304 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-z \\\\ x-y+z \\\\ 3z-6x\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3e25176-30ac-5743-87ee-ef074c94ef6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351304 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $3$rd one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364968 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation\n $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-x-4z \\\\ 2x+7z-6y \\\\ 16z+4x-12y\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c099173-4895-55c8-a699-8c50ffc5eadb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364968 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the 2nd one).  Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] 